"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[194],{2194:function(e,n,t){t.r(n),n.default='# Emergent 2D Cell Locomotion with Actin Polymerization\n\n<img width= "90%" src="/images/actin_header.png" alt="actin polymerization header">\n\n## Preface\nThis project was done together by [Grisha Szep](https://gszep.com/en/) and me in 2025, from spring to autumn.\nOur goal was to work together, and create an interesting looking interactive simulation of emergent cell locomotion with WebGPU.\nGrisha has background in physics and a PhD in computational biology, and both of us love artistic projects.\nLooking back, the way we handled self-assembly via texture writing feels like the heart of the project. It wasn\'t the most obvious approach, but it ended up being the most rewarding puzzle to solve. \n\nBeyond the code, it was great to tackle this together. Working with Grisha taught me things about the intersection of biology and physics that I never would have picked up on my own.\n\nWithout further ado, here is a preview of the result:\n\n<iframe width="560" height="315" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/DlsEYlpGbbA" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe>\n\n\n# Background\n## Bringing biology to life\nLearning about complex bio-chemical pathways when I was a neurobiology student was fascinating. I remember being even more fascinated about imaging how things would _look_ when all clicked together as a working machine: All the colorful proteins clicking together and flowing through the crazy machinery of nature.\n\n## Single-cell locomotion\nWe chose to focus on cell locomotion. Specifically cell locomotion based on actin polymerization, as seen in eukaryotic cells, like the zebrafish’s.\n\nThe mechanism is as follows:\nActin proteins are floating around the cellular fluid (cytoplasma). They’re refered to as monomers. They can self-assemble: binding to eachother (polymerize) to form longer actin polymers. The actin particle can unbind from the polymer as well. Polymerization and depolymerization are triggered by actin-binding proteins.\n\nWhat allows this process functionality is that binding can only happen at one end of the chain (the + end) and depolymerization at the other (-) end. This mechanism is called threadmilling, and allows growth into one direction.\n\nCell movement emerges from these stiff polymers pushing the cell membrane outwards.\n\n## Our GPU solution\nAnd now for the technical details:\nWe chose to model the cell in 2D, since 3D isn’t strictly necessary to model this phenomenon. As a bonus, a 2D cell slice is much easier to see what\'s happening inside.\n\nTo make things scale and interactive, we forced ourselves to prevent exponential scaling of our simulation with environment or particle number.\n\n### Segments as linked lists\n\nThe fundamental unit of the simulation is a segment. Both actin filaments and the cell membrane are built from segments. Each is modeled as a particles that lives in GPU storage buffers, storing a 2D position, an orientation, and two neighbor indices: head and tail. This makes every filament a linked list on the GPU. Whether a segment is a free monomer, the plus-end of a growing polymer, or an interior link. All of that is encoded purely by which neighbors it points to. The membrane is the same data structure, just a separate circular chain.\n\n### Threadmilling\n\n<img width= "70%" src="/images/actin_simple.png" alt="actin simulation diagram">\n\nA simplified view of what\'s happening. The membrane is blue, actin monomers are green, polymers are red. The white dots are the sensors of the polymer\'s head particle, scanning for nearby monomers to bind. The purple field is the area where a binding event can happen.\n\nEach step, free monomers search for nearby polymer plus-ends and bind with a small probability. Minus-ends detach with another small probability. Together this yields threadmilling: filaments grow at one end and shrink at the other, recycling monomers continuously.\n\nPolymerization is not uniform across space. A 2D signal field centered on the cell membrane modulates the local binding rate: Where the signal is high, actin grows faster.\nThis probability field is modulated by the cell-membrane which activates it whenever a \'food source\' is closeby. In our case we just use a mouseclick/drag as the foodsource.\n\n### Physics with position-based dynamics\n\nOnce segments form chains, they need to behave like stiff filaments. We use extended position-based dynamics (XPBD) to enforce distance constraints (keeping consecutive segments at a fixed rest length) and bending constraints (pulling each segment toward the midpoint of its neighbors). The compliance parameter controls filament stiffness: Stiffer filaments push more forcefully against the membrane. Both constraints run in multiple parallel passes per frame, using an alternating pass-ID scheme so neighboring segments never update simultaneously (Gaus-seidel strategy, opposed to Jacobi, where everything is simultaneously updated).\n\n### Spatial lookup via textures\n\nThe classic bottleneck in particle simulations is neighbor-finding. Rather than building spatial hash tables, we use the GPU\'s texture hardware directly. Each frame, every segment writes its ID into a 2D index texture at its position. Lookups become O(1): to find what\'s near a point, just sample the texture. Toroidal wrapping handles boundaries.\n\nA second set of texture layers tracks steric density: How crowded each region is with monomers, polymers, or membrane. These fields are Gaussian-blurred each frame, which makes particles repel each other at short range without explicit pairwise force calculations.\n\n### The membrane\n\nThe membrane is a circular chain of segments that deforms under pressure from polymerizing actin. This deformation is emergent: as filaments grow and stiffen against it, individual membrane segments get pushed outward, and the bending constraints propagate this into a smooth protrusion. protrusion on one part of the cell pulls the rest of the cell with it, or in other words, the cell is locomoting.\n\nWe even did an extra segway into vesicle budding, by letting membrane segments also detect other segments nearby via texture lookup. This could split the cell (mitosis) or break off a vesical, all by the basic mechanism of splitting one loop into two.\n\nAnyway, It’s been an incredible deep-dive into biology and emergent systems—thanks for reading!'}}]);